From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 23:43:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] download: fail a post-message that is gone from the
 server
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A download-on-demand message whose post-message the server no longer has
(a chatmail relay deletes messages after a while) stayed in
`DownloadState::InProgress` forever: the UI showed a spinner that never
finished and never offered the retry button.

`download_msg()` finds no `imap` row for the message and, before
returning the error, deletes the `available_post_msgs` entry — the very
flag `download_msgs()` uses to tell "the server had it and it is gone
now" (-> `Failure`) from "the post-message has not arrived yet" (-> keep
waiting). With the flag erased the caller always took the second branch
and also kept the `download` row, so every fetch cycle retried the same
lookup and re-decided to wait.

The location-unknown branch now checks that flag before clearing it: if
the post-message was announced as available, the message is set to
`Failure` and dropped from the download queue, so the bubble offers a
retry (which re-queues it, and prefetch re-queues it by itself if the
message reappears on the server). A post-message that has not arrived
yet was never in `available_post_msgs` and keeps waiting as before.

The tradeoff is a message moved out of the watched folder in the same
IMAP iteration that runs the download: its `imap` row is deleted by the
move and only recreated when the target folder is prefetched, so a
download requested inside that window now fails once instead of
completing by itself a moment later. It costs one tap on retry, and it
only affects setups that move messages at all (chatmail does not).

Our chunked download path had the same dead end for a different reason:
when `receive_imf` rejects the assembled message it reports the device
message and returns `Done`, which makes the caller drop the queue entry
while the message keeps its `InProgress` state. It is set to `Failure`
there too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/download.rs | 80 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 79 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/download.rs b/src/download.rs
index fb971c0..f5808c4 100644
--- a/src/download.rs
+++ b/src/download.rs
@@ -464,7 +464,7 @@ pub(crate) async fn download_msg(
 
     let Some((server_uid, server_folder, msg_transport_id)) = row else {
         // No IMAP record found, we don't know the UID and folder.
-        delete_from_available_post_msgs(context, &rfc724_mid).await?;
+        post_msg_location_unknown(context, &rfc724_mid).await?;
         return Err(anyhow!(
             "IMAP location for {rfc724_mid:?} post-message is unknown"
         ));
@@ -638,6 +638,11 @@ async fn fetch_single_msg(
                 let mut msg = Message::new_text(text);
                 add_device_msg(context, None, Some(&mut msg)).await?;
                 staging.delete().await?;
+                // The caller drops the download queue entry for `Done`, so
+                // without this the message would keep its `InProgress` state
+                // with nothing left to move it on: a spinner that never ends
+                // and no button to retry it with.
+                set_state_to_failure(context, &rfc724_mid).await?;
             }
             Ok(_) => {
                 staging.delete().await?;
@@ -721,6 +726,26 @@ async fn set_state_to_failure(context: &Context, rfc724_mid: &str) -> Result<()>
     Ok(())
 }
 
+/// Handles a post-message whose IMAP location we do not know.
+///
+/// If it was recorded as available, it was on the server and is gone now — a
+/// chatmail relay deletes messages, a UID gets expunged. That location never
+/// comes back, so the message is failed here and taken out of the download
+/// queue; otherwise every fetch cycle would retry it and the UI would show
+/// "downloading" forever. Pressing download again re-queues it, and if the
+/// message does reappear on the server, prefetch queues it by itself.
+///
+/// A post-message that has merely not arrived yet was never recorded as
+/// available; it keeps waiting in the queue as before.
+async fn post_msg_location_unknown(context: &Context, rfc724_mid: &str) -> Result<()> {
+    if available_post_msgs_contains_rfc724_mid(context, rfc724_mid).await? {
+        set_state_to_failure(context, rfc724_mid).await?;
+        delete_from_downloads(context, rfc724_mid).await?;
+    }
+    delete_from_available_post_msgs(context, rfc724_mid).await?;
+    Ok(())
+}
+
 async fn available_post_msgs_contains_rfc724_mid(
     context: &Context,
     rfc724_mid: &str,
@@ -914,6 +939,59 @@ async fn test_update_download_state() -> Result<()> {
         Ok(())
     }
 
+    async fn download_contains(t: &TestContext, rfc724_mid: &str) -> Result<bool> {
+        Ok(t.sql
+            .query_get_value::<String>(
+                "SELECT rfc724_mid FROM download WHERE rfc724_mid=?",
+                (rfc724_mid,),
+            )
+            .await?
+            .is_some())
+    }
+
+    /// A post-message that is gone from the server must fail instead of being
+    /// retried forever, but one that has not arrived yet must keep waiting.
+    #[tokio::test(flavor = "multi_thread", worker_threads = 2)]
+    async fn test_post_msg_location_unknown() -> Result<()> {
+        let mut tcm = TestContextManager::new();
+        let (t, msg_id) = real_msg_id(&mut tcm).await?;
+        let mid = "post-msg@example.org";
+        t.sql
+            .execute("UPDATE msgs SET rfc724_mid=? WHERE id=?", (mid, msg_id))
+            .await?;
+        msg_id
+            .update_download_state(&t, DownloadState::InProgress)
+            .await?;
+        t.sql
+            .execute(
+                "INSERT INTO download (rfc724_mid, msg_id) VALUES (?,?)",
+                (mid, msg_id),
+            )
+            .await?;
+
+        // Never seen on the server: it may still arrive, so it stays queued.
+        post_msg_location_unknown(&t, mid).await?;
+        assert_eq!(
+            Message::load_from_db(&t, msg_id).await?.download_state(),
+            DownloadState::InProgress
+        );
+        assert!(download_contains(&t, mid).await?);
+
+        // Was on the server, gone now: fail it and stop retrying.
+        t.sql
+            .execute("INSERT INTO available_post_msgs VALUES (?)", (mid,))
+            .await?;
+        post_msg_location_unknown(&t, mid).await?;
+        assert_eq!(
+            Message::load_from_db(&t, msg_id).await?.download_state(),
+            DownloadState::Failure
+        );
+        assert!(!download_contains(&t, mid).await?);
+        assert!(!available_post_msgs_contains_rfc724_mid(&t, mid).await?);
+
+        Ok(())
+    }
+
     /// Deterministic fake message bytes: assembly bugs (gaps, duplication,
     /// reordering) show up as a mismatch against this pattern.
     fn fake_message(len: usize) -> Vec<u8> {
